think Read the pin before folding rather than trusting the preview. 80 table rows, 78 carrying a stamp, and 73 of the link targets pointing at yonder/ rather than date/.
think That looked like REDS %200's second sub-fault waiting to happen -- 21 immutable shelves carrying 73 links that resolve nowhere. Read shelf_row in tools/rye/session_logs_archive.rye instead of guessing: it prefixes ../ to every relative target and shortens only date/, which is exactly right from a shelf one directory down.
think Verified by doing anyway. All 120 links across the 21 written shelves resolve on disk.
obs The fold left the living table with a header, a delimiter, and no rows, sitting below the room's closing benediction -- the shape REDS %200 named as wanting a hand. Rewrote the front door: a shelf table holding the way in with per-day counts, the living table beneath it, and the benediction back as the last word.
obs Two flat dated prompts sat in the room with no rows at all, so the pin under-described the room it claims to describe. Seated rows for both, which is what index_follows_room actually promises.
obs The pin had stood 27 bytes over the 24,576 its own header declares. It reads 9,797 now, under the bound for the first time.
obs Moving the ceiling 119 -> 41 red the control until its planted counts moved with it, 119/120 -> 41/42, and the witness assertions quoting those strings moved too. That is REDS %200's own lesson arriving as routine rather than as a fault.
